{
  "title": "{ip:Credo} che la ragazza che {ip:parla} {csp:sia} Anna",
  "translation": "I think the girl who is speaking is Anna",
  "topics": "Giving opinions and preferences, linking words, the relative pronoun che",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Giving an opinion"
      },
      "The simplest way uses an ordinary tense: *Secondo me {ip:è} interessante* (In my opinion it's interesting). *Per me* works the same way.",
      "After *{ip:penso} che* and *{ip:credo} che*, Italian uses a special verb form, the *congiuntivo*. You will study it properly at a later level. For now, just recognise the most common forms: *{csp:sia}* (is), *{csp:abbia}* (has), *{imv:vada}* (goes).",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Without congiuntivo",
            "With congiuntivo"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Secondo me {ip:è} difficile.",
              "{ip:Penso} che {csp:**sia**} difficile."
            ],
            [
              "Per me Luca {ip:ha} ragione.",
              "{ip:Credo} che Luca {csp:**abbia**} ragione."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "If the person who thinks is the same as the one who acts, use *di* with an infinitive: *{ip:Penso} di partire domani*.",
      {
        "h": "Saying what you prefer"
      },
      "*Preferire* follows the pattern of *capire*, with *-isc-*: *{ip:preferisco}, {ip:preferisci}, {ip:preferisce}, {ip:preferiamo}, {ip:preferite}, {ip:preferiscono}*. It can be followed by a noun or a verb: *{ip:Preferisco} il tè. {ip:Preferisco} restare a casa.*",
      {
        "h": "Linking ideas"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Word",
            "Meaning",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "però",
              "but, however",
              "{ip:È} caro, però {ip:è} bello."
            ],
            [
              "quindi",
              "so",
              "{ip:Piove}, quindi {ip:resto} a casa."
            ],
            [
              "allora",
              "so, then",
              "{ip:Sei} stanco? Allora {imv:riposati}."
            ],
            [
              "mentre",
              "while",
              "Mentre {ip:mangio}, {ip:leggo}."
            ],
            [
              "quando",
              "when",
              "Quando {ip:arrivo}, ti {ip:chiamo}."
            ],
            [
              "perché",
              "because",
              "Non {ip:esco} perché {ip:piove}."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "To join two actions done by the same person, use these with an infinitive.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Form",
            "Example",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "prima di + infinitive",
              "Prima di uscire, {ip:mangio}.",
              "Before going out, I eat."
            ],
            [
              "dopo aver + participle",
              "Dopo aver mangiato, {ip:esco}.",
              "After eating, I go out."
            ],
            [
              "dopo essere + participle",
              "Dopo essere arrivata, {pp:ho chiamato}.",
              "After arriving, I called."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "Che joins two sentences"
      },
      "*Che* replaces the noun that the two sentences share. It works for people and things and never changes. In English you can sometimes leave out *who* or *that*, but in Italian *che* must always be there.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Two sentences",
            "One sentence"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "La ragazza {ip:parla}. {ip:È} mia sorella.",
              "La ragazza **che** {ip:parla} {ip:è} mia sorella."
            ],
            [
              "{ip:Leggo} un libro. Il libro {ip:è} bello.",
              "Il libro **che** {ip:leggo} {ip:è} bello."
            ]
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
